# Lock-Free MPMC Queue with Epoch-Based Reclamation

The classic Michael-Scott queue is the natural fit here: an unbounded singly-linked list with a permanent "dummy" head, where producers CAS onto `tail.next` and consumers CAS `head` forward. The hard part isn't the algorithm, it's safe memory reclamation. A naive `Box::from_raw` after a successful pop leads to use-after-free (and ABA) because another thread may still be reading that node.

I'll pair the queue with a hand-rolled epoch-based reclamation (EBR) scheme inspired by `crossbeam-epoch`:

- A global epoch counter and three garbage buckets.
- Each thread registers a `Participant` and "pins" itself by writing the current global epoch into a thread-local slot. While pinned, no node observed via the queue can be reclaimed.
- A retired node goes into `garbage[current_epoch % 3]`.
- Periodically, a thread tries to advance the global epoch. If every pinned participant is at the current epoch, the bucket two epochs old is provably unreachable and can be freed.

Three buckets is the minimum: while we're advancing from `e` to `e+1`, threads can be pinned at either of those two, so only garbage from `e-2` is safe to drop.

## Implementation

```rust
//! Lock-free MPMC queue (Michael-Scott) with epoch-based reclamation.

use std::cell::Cell;
use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::mem::MaybeUninit;
use std::ptr;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicPtr, AtomicUsize, Ordering};
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex, OnceLock};

// ============================================================
// Epoch-Based Reclamation
// ============================================================

const NUM_EPOCHS: usize = 3;
const UNPINNED: usize = usize::MAX;
const ADVANCE_INTERVAL: usize = 64;

/// Per-thread state shared with the collector.
struct Participant {
    /// Either `UNPINNED` or the epoch at which this thread is pinned.
    local_epoch: AtomicUsize,
}

impl Participant {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self { local_epoch: AtomicUsize::new(UNPINNED) }
    }
}

/// Wrapper used to pass raw pointers across the `Send` bound on deferred
/// closures. The pointer's `T` must itself be `Send`.
struct SendPtr<T>(*mut T);
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for SendPtr<T> {}

type Deferred = Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send>;

struct Collector {
    global_epoch: AtomicUsize,
    garbage: [Mutex<Vec<Deferred>>; NUM_EPOCHS],
    participants: Mutex<Vec<Arc<Participant>>>,
    pin_counter: AtomicUsize,
}

impl Collector {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            global_epoch: AtomicUsize::new(0),
            garbage: [
                Mutex::new(Vec::new()),
                Mutex::new(Vec::new()),
                Mutex::new(Vec::new()),
            ],
            participants: Mutex::new(Vec::new()),
            pin_counter: AtomicUsize::new(0),
        }
    }

    fn register(&self) -> Arc<Participant> {
        let p = Arc::new(Participant::new());
        self.participants.lock().unwrap().push(p.clone());
        p
    }

    fn retire(&self, epoch: usize, f: Deferred) {
        self.garbage[epoch % NUM_EPOCHS].lock().unwrap().push(f);
    }

    /// Try to advance the global epoch and reclaim the oldest bucket.
    ///
    /// Correctness sketch: if every pinned participant is at `global`, then
    /// after we bump the epoch to `global + 1` future pins land at
    /// `global + 1`. Hence no live thread holds a pointer retired at
    /// `global - 2` or earlier, and that bucket may be freed.
    fn try_advance(&self) {
        let global = self.global_epoch.load(Ordering::SeqCst);

        {
            let participants = self.participants.lock().unwrap();
            for p in participants.iter() {
                let e = p.local_epoch.load(Ordering::SeqCst);
                if e != UNPINNED && e != global {
                    return;
                }
            }
        }

        let new_epoch = global.wrapping_add(1);
        if self
            .global_epoch
            .compare_exchange(global, new_epoch, Ordering::SeqCst, Ordering::SeqCst)
            .is_err()
        {
            return;
        }

        // (new_epoch - 2) mod 3 == (new_epoch + 1) mod 3
        let stale = new_epoch.wrapping_add(1) % NUM_EPOCHS;
        let drained: Vec<Deferred> = {
            let mut g = self.garbage[stale].lock().unwrap();
            std::mem::take(&mut *g)
        };
        for f in drained {
            f();
        }
    }
}

static COLLECTOR: OnceLock<Collector> = OnceLock::new();

fn collector() -> &'static Collector {
    COLLECTOR.get_or_init(Collector::new)
}

thread_local! {
    static PARTICIPANT: Arc<Participant> = collector().register();
    static PIN_DEPTH:   Cell<usize>      = const { Cell::new(0) };
}

/// RAII guard. While alive, all pointers reachable from the queue are
/// guaranteed not to be reclaimed.
pub struct Guard {
    epoch: usize,
    // Guards must not move between threads.
    _not_send: PhantomData<*mut ()>,
}

impl Guard {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let coll = collector();
        let depth = PIN_DEPTH.with(|d| {
            let v = d.get();
            d.set(v + 1);
            v
        });

        let epoch = if depth == 0 {
            let e = coll.global_epoch.load(Ordering::SeqCst);
            PARTICIPANT.with(|p| p.local_epoch.store(e, Ordering::SeqCst));

            // Amortized advancement attempt.
            let n = coll.pin_counter.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
            if n % ADVANCE_INTERVAL == 0 {
                coll.try_advance();
            }
            e
        } else {
            PARTICIPANT.with(|p| p.local_epoch.load(Ordering::Relaxed))
        };

        Guard { epoch, _not_send: PhantomData }
    }

    /// Schedule `f` to run once the current epoch is no longer reachable.
    pub fn defer<F: FnOnce() + Send + 'static>(&self, f: F) {
        collector().retire(self.epoch, Box::new(f));
    }
}

impl Drop for Guard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let new_depth = PIN_DEPTH.with(|d| {
            let v = d.get() - 1;
            d.set(v);
            v
        });
        if new_depth == 0 {
            PARTICIPANT.with(|p| p.local_epoch.store(UNPINNED, Ordering::SeqCst));
        }
    }
}

pub fn pin() -> Guard { Guard::new() }

// ============================================================
// Lock-Free MPMC Queue (Michael-Scott)
// ============================================================

struct Node<T> {
    /// Uninit on the dummy node and on nodes whose value has been moved out.
    value: MaybeUninit<T>,
    next:  AtomicPtr<Node<T>>,
}

pub struct MpmcQueue<T> {
    head: AtomicPtr<Node<T>>,
    tail: AtomicPtr<Node<T>>,
}

impl<T: Send + 'static> MpmcQueue<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        let dummy = Box::into_raw(Box::new(Node {
            value: MaybeUninit::uninit(),
            next:  AtomicPtr::new(ptr::null_mut()),
        }));
        Self {
            head: AtomicPtr::new(dummy),
            tail: AtomicPtr::new(dummy),
        }
    }

    /// Enqueue `value`. Lock-free: a thread that fails its CAS observes
    /// some other thread having made progress.
    pub fn push(&self, value: T) {
        let new_node = Box::into_raw(Box::new(Node {
            value: MaybeUninit::new(value),
            next:  AtomicPtr::new(ptr::null_mut()),
        }));

        let _g = pin();
        loop {
            let tail     = self.tail.load(Ordering::Acquire);
            // Safe: pin keeps `tail` alive.
            let tail_ref = unsafe { &*tail };
            let next     = tail_ref.next.load(Ordering::Acquire);

            // Detect torn (tail, next) snapshot.
            if tail != self.tail.load(Ordering::Acquire) {
                continue;
            }

            if next.is_null() {
                // Try to splice on the new node.
                if tail_ref
                    .next
                    .compare_exchange(ptr::null_mut(), new_node,
                                      Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire)
                    .is_ok()
                {
                    // Best-effort tail swing; another thread may help.
                    let _ = self.tail.compare_exchange(
                        tail, new_node, Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire);
                    return;
                }
            } else {
                // Tail is lagging; help it forward.
                let _ = self.tail.compare_exchange(
                    tail, next, Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire);
            }
        }
    }

    /// Dequeue an item, or `None` if empty.
    pub fn pop(&self) -> Option<T> {
        let g = pin();
        loop {
            let head     = self.head.load(Ordering::Acquire);
            let tail     = self.tail.load(Ordering::Acquire);
            let head_ref = unsafe { &*head };
            let next     = head_ref.next.load(Ordering::Acquire);

            if head != self.head.load(Ordering::Acquire) {
                continue;
            }

            if head == tail {
                if next.is_null() {
                    return None;             // Empty.
                }
                // Tail is lagging; help it forward, then retry.
                let _ = self.tail.compare_exchange(
                    tail, next, Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire);
                continue;
            }

            // Race for the right to consume `next`.
            if self.head
                .compare_exchange(head, next, Ordering::AcqRel, Ordering::Acquire)
                .is_ok()
            {
                // We won. `next` is now the new dummy and we are the only
                // thread that will ever read its value (subsequent poppers
                // will read `next.next`'s value). The pin keeps `next` alive
                // long enough for this read.
                let next_ref = unsafe { &*next };
                let value    = unsafe { next_ref.value.as_ptr().read() };

                // Old dummy `head` is unreachable. Its value is uninit, so
                // we only need to free the box. ABA is prevented because we
                // defer the free until no thread can hold the old pointer.
                let head_send = SendPtr(head);
                g.defer(move || unsafe {
                    drop(Box::from_raw(head_send.0));
                });
                return Some(value);
            }
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for MpmcQueue<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        // Owned uniquely here. Drop every live value, deallocate every node.
        unsafe {
            let mut cur      = *self.head.get_mut();
            let mut is_dummy = true;
            while !cur.is_null() {
                let mut boxed = Box::from_raw(cur);
                let next      = *boxed.next.get_mut();
                if !is_dummy {
                    boxed.value.assume_init_drop();
                }
                is_dummy = false;
                cur      = next;
            }
        }
    }
}

unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for MpmcQueue<T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Send> Sync for MpmcQueue<T> {}
```

## Usage and a stress test

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let q: Arc<MpmcQueue<u64>> = Arc::new(MpmcQueue::new());

    const PRODUCERS:    usize = 4;
    const CONSUMERS:    usize = 4;
    const PER_PRODUCER: u64   = 100_000;

    let mut handles = Vec::new();

    for p in 0..PRODUCERS {
        let q = Arc::clone(&q);
        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
            for i in 0..PER_PRODUCER {
                q.push(p as u64 * PER_PRODUCER + i);
            }
        }));
    }

    let total = (PRODUCERS as u64) * PER_PRODUCER;
    let counters: Vec<_> = (0..CONSUMERS).map(|_| Arc::new(AtomicU64::new(0))).collect();
    for c in 0..CONSUMERS {
        let q   = Arc::clone(&q);
        let cnt = Arc::clone(&counters[c]);
        handles.push(thread::spawn(move || {
            loop {
                if let Some(_v) = q.pop() {
                    cnt.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
                } else if cnt.load(Ordering::Relaxed) == 0 {
                    thread::yield_now();
                } else {
                    // Crude termination: keep draining until producers finish
                    // (in real code you'd use a sentinel / shutdown flag).
                    thread::yield_now();
                }
                // For demo purposes, exit once all producers can't possibly
                // have more items. (Replace with a proper shutdown protocol.)
                if counters.iter().map(|c| c.load(Ordering::Relaxed)).sum::<u64>() == total {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }));
    }

    for h in handles { h.join().unwrap(); }
}

use std::sync::atomic::AtomicU64;
```

## Notes and trade-offs worth flagging

- **Why `SeqCst` on the epoch fields?** Pin/advance correctness depends on the total order between a participant's epoch store and the collector's epoch CAS. A relaxed implementation is possible (crossbeam uses fences) but harder to reason about. Trading a small amount of throughput for a clearly-correct skeleton is a reasonable starting point.
- **Read after CAS in `pop`.** Reading `next.value` after the successful `head` CAS is safe because the new head is a dummy that no other consumer will ever consume from. Reading before the CAS would force a `Copy` bound or risk double-drops on retry.
- **Drop semantics.** `MaybeUninit<T>` does not auto-drop, so retired nodes can be freed with a plain `Box::from_raw` without touching their value. The queue's own `Drop` walks the chain and explicitly drops the live values.
- **Thread exit while pinned.** A thread that is pinned at exit blocks epoch advancement permanently. RAII guards prevent this in normal flow; aborting threads are still a hazard. A production EBR (crossbeam) handles this with collector handoff and forced unpin.
- **Throughput pitfalls of the M-S queue.** Both producers and consumers contend on a small set of cache lines (`head`, `tail`, the latest node's `next`). For high core counts you'd reach for a Vyukov bounded ring or a segmented queue, but the algorithmic structure of EBR remains the same.
- **Out of scope here.** No hazard pointers, no `try_pop_n`, no backoff, no shutdown protocol on the queue itself. Adding exponential backoff inside the CAS loops typically yields a meaningful win on contended workloads.
